1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,266
Eighteen human beings have died flying toward space.

2
00:00:03,266 --> 00:00:06,199
Every single one of them was brought home.

3
00:00:06,199 --> 00:00:10,599
This is the story of exactly how, and what it actually took.

4
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:14,981
Some were burned before they ever left the launch pad.

5
00:00:14,981 --> 00:00:19,529
Some were found still strapped into their seats, exactly where they had been sitting.

6
00:00:19,529 --> 00:00:24,402
And one famous story about how a man died turns out to be completely invented.

7
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:27,543
Five disasters, spanning almost forty years. Twenty one people.

8
00:00:27,543 --> 00:00:31,116
Eighteen of them died in the vacuum of space itself, three never even left the ground.

9
00:00:31,116 --> 00:00:34,689
This is the honestly sourced story of their recovery, their myths, and where they rest today.

10
00:00:34,689 --> 00:00:36,922
Chapter four is the one Hollywood got the most wrong.

11
00:00:38,066 --> 00:00:44,257
We start at the very beginning, before a single rocket carrying this program's astronauts had even left the ground.

12
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,031
Chapter one. The tragedy before the first launch.

13
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:51,474
January 27th, 1967.

14
00:00:51,474 --> 00:01:01,064
Astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White and Roger Chaffee are sealed inside their Apollo capsule at Cape Kennedy, running a routine countdown rehearsal.

15
00:01:01,064 --> 00:01:04,115
The rocket underneath them isn't even fueled.

16
00:01:05,233 --> 00:01:08,493
The cabin is pressurized with almost pure oxygen.

17
00:01:08,493 --> 00:01:15,421
A spark somewhere in the wiring starts a fire, and in that atmosphere, it spreads almost instantly.

18
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:22,855
The capsule's hatch opens inward, in three sealed layers, designed to be held shut by internal pressure.

19
00:01:22,855 --> 00:01:28,034
Once the fire raised that pressure, no one alive could have pulled it open.

20
00:01:29,166 --> 00:01:33,808
The pad crew worked for over five minutes to get the last hatch layer off.

21
00:01:33,808 --> 00:01:37,212
By the time they succeeded, the three astronauts were already gone.

22
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:43,557
The official finding was death by asphyxiation, from breathing in toxic gases.

23
00:01:43,557 --> 00:01:48,782
The burns came after. Investigators said unconsciousness would have come on rapidly.

24
00:01:49,899 --> 00:01:58,660
It took nearly six hours before their bodies could be recovered, since the heat had fused parts of their suits directly to the cabin.

25
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,850
Air Force pathologists conducted the autopsies.

26
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:03,770
The full reports have never been made public.

27
00:02:04,899 --> 00:02:13,557
Investigators traced the fire to a general area near a damaged wire bundle, but they could never conclusively identify the exact spark.

28
00:02:13,557 --> 00:02:17,098
Officially, the true ignition point may never be known.

29
00:02:18,233 --> 00:02:21,514
Grissom and Chaffee are buried at Arlington National Cemetery.

30
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:26,984
White is buried at West Point, the academy he loved, at his own family's request.

31
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:34,260
That was the cost of testing on the ground, before a single astronaut in this program had even reached orbit.

32
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:38,242
The next disaster came from the country racing hardest to beat it there.

33
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:42,683
Chapter two. The flight that never had a chance.

34
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:45,393
April 23rd, 1967.

35
00:02:45,393 --> 00:02:55,797
Cosmonaut Vladimir Komarov launches alone aboard Soyuz 1, a brand new, rushed spacecraft that had failed its last unmanned tests.

36
00:02:56,933 --> 00:03:00,824
After a day fighting a crippled spacecraft, Komarov brings it down for reentry.

37
00:03:00,824 --> 00:03:08,009
The main parachute fails to deploy properly, and when the reserve chute deploys, its lines tangle in the drogue chute still trailing above it.

38
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:14,921
The descent module hits the ground at roughly ninety miles an hour and catches fire on impact.

39
00:03:14,921 --> 00:03:19,007
Komarov becomes the first human being to die during an actual spaceflight.

40
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:26,440
You may have heard that a listening post overseas caught him screaming and cursing in his final minutes.

41
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:34,500
That story comes from a single, unconfirmed source in one book, and named historians have since debunked it using the real archived transcripts.

42
00:03:35,633 --> 00:03:47,014
What those transcripts actually show is a calm, professional cosmonaut, working the problem until the very end, and signing off with something close to, I feel excellent, everything's in order.

43
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:53,133
By the accounts of Soviet officials at the scene, Komarov's remains were badly damaged in the crash and fire.

44
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:58,660
He was given a state funeral in Moscow, cremated, and his ashes were placed in the Kremlin Wall, in Red Square.

45
00:03:59,799 --> 00:04:01,623
One man had died coming home.

46
00:04:01,623 --> 00:04:06,486
Four years later, three more would die before they ever made it back to Earth's surface.

47
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:11,839
Chapter three. The only humans to die in space itself.

48
00:04:12,966 --> 00:04:14,306
June 30th, 1971.

49
00:04:14,306 --> 00:04:26,367
Cosmonauts Georgi Dobrovolsky, Vladislav Volkov and Viktor Patsayev are heading home after a record breaking twenty three days aboard Salyut 1, the first space station in history.

50
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:31,757
During separation, twelve small explosive devices are supposed to fire in sequence.

51
00:04:31,757 --> 00:04:38,498
Instead they fire all at once, and the shock jars open a pressure valve that should have stayed shut.

52
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:46,651
That valve opens at an altitude of one hundred sixty eight kilometers, well above the internationally recognized edge of space.

53
00:04:46,651 --> 00:04:50,160
The cabin is now open to the vacuum outside it.

54
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:54,147
The three men are not wearing pressure suits.

55
00:04:54,147 --> 00:04:59,841
There simply wasn't room, after engineers squeezed a third seat into a capsule built for two.

56
00:04:59,841 --> 00:05:02,688
Their cabin fully depressurizes in under a minute.

57
00:05:03,833 --> 00:05:07,856
Evidence suggests Patsayev tried to manually seal the valve by hand.

58
00:05:07,856 --> 00:05:12,244
Even done perfectly, that fix would have taken closer to a minute.

59
00:05:12,244 --> 00:05:14,439
It was already far too slow.

60
00:05:15,566 --> 00:05:17,494
The capsule itself came down completely undamaged, right on target.

61
00:05:17,494 --> 00:05:20,578
When recovery crews opened the hatch, they found all three men still strapped into their seats.

62
00:05:20,578 --> 00:05:25,205
Recovery accounts describe visible discoloration and signs of bleeding, real evidence of what the decompression had done, even though the capsule itself was untouched.

63
00:05:26,333 --> 00:05:30,565
The cause of death was asphyxiation from the sudden loss of pressure.

64
00:05:30,565 --> 00:05:39,734
Investigators found bleeding in the brain and inner ear, caused by dissolved gases boiling out of the blood, the same mechanism that gives divers the bends.

65
00:05:40,866 --> 00:05:44,480
Useful consciousness would have been lost within about thirty seconds.

66
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:51,707
All three men were given a state funeral, and their ashes were placed in the Kremlin Wall, near Yuri Gagarin.

67
00:05:52,833 --> 00:05:56,838
American astronaut Tom Stafford served as a pallbearer, representing President Nixon.

68
00:05:56,838 --> 00:06:04,484
And to this day, these three remain the only human beings confirmed to have died above the edge of space itself.

69
00:06:05,633 --> 00:06:14,071
After this, Soyuz permanently reverted to carrying only two cosmonauts at a time, specifically so there would always be room for pressure suits again.

70
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:19,643
It would be fifteen years before another spacecraft failure killed its crew.

71
00:06:19,643 --> 00:06:23,716
This time, the entire world would be watching it happen live.

72
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:28,804
Chapter four. The disaster the whole world watched.

73
00:06:29,933 --> 00:06:31,135
January 28th, 1986.

74
00:06:31,135 --> 00:06:42,759
Seventy three seconds after launch, a failed rubber seal lets flame burn through the side of the Space Shuttle Challenger's external fuel tank, and the vehicle tears itself apart.

75
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:46,637
But the crew cabin itself does not explode.

76
00:06:46,637 --> 00:06:54,506
It separates from the wreckage largely intact, and its own momentum carries it thousands of feet higher before it finally starts to fall.

77
00:06:55,633 --> 00:07:01,807
It falls for close to three minutes before hitting the ocean at more than two hundred miles an hour.

78
00:07:01,807 --> 00:07:03,757
That impact alone was completely unsurvivable.

79
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,839
Here is the part almost every other video about this gets wrong.

80
00:07:08,839 --> 00:07:19,346
NASA's own official finding was that the exact cause of death could not be positively determined, and whether the crew lost consciousness during that fall is possible, but not certain, either way.

81
00:07:20,466 --> 00:07:26,665
Some emergency air packs had been manually switched on, and some cockpit switches had been thrown by hand.

82
00:07:26,665 --> 00:07:30,454
That is real physical evidence of crew action after the breakup.

83
00:07:30,454 --> 00:07:33,553
It still does not settle the question either way.

84
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:41,296
What follows is one of the largest search operations in Navy history, covering hundreds of square miles of ocean.

85
00:07:41,296 --> 00:07:45,462
The crew cabin is finally located on the seafloor in early March.

86
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,515
Recovery continues for weeks.

87
00:07:48,515 --> 00:07:54,741
Remains are identified through dental records, using the forensic methods available in 1986.

88
00:07:54,741 --> 00:07:59,531
Some remains could never be attributed to one specific person.

89
00:08:00,666 --> 00:08:08,709
That unidentifiable portion was cremated and buried together at Arlington National Cemetery, beneath a memorial dedicated the following year.

90
00:08:08,709 --> 00:08:11,672
Some crew members are buried there individually.

91
00:08:11,672 --> 00:08:17,175
Others rest at their own family's chosen cemeteries, from New Hampshire to Hawaii.

92
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:21,616
And here is the myth we have to correct.

93
00:08:21,616 --> 00:08:30,460
A widely shared "final transcript" of the crew talking calmly as they fell first appeared in a tabloid years later, and it is fabricated.

94
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:35,619
The real last words, from the actual flight recorder, were just two: uh oh.

95
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:45,752
Seventeen years after Challenger, on the very same kind of vehicle, the story would repeat itself, this time on the way back down instead of the way up.

96
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:49,669
Chapter five. When it happened again.

97
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:52,244
February 1st, 2003.

98
00:08:52,244 --> 00:09:00,909
The Space Shuttle Columbia breaks apart during reentry, high over Texas, traveling eighteen times the speed of sound.

99
00:09:02,033 --> 00:09:08,271
Wreckage falls across a corridor stretching more than two hundred miles, from eastern Texas into Louisiana.

100
00:09:08,271 --> 00:09:12,560
Search teams recover more than eighty thousand individual pieces of debris.

101
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:21,171
All seven crew members are positively identified within two weeks, through DNA testing cross checked against dental and medical records.

102
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:25,993
A later NASA investigation concluded the accident was not survivable.

103
00:09:25,993 --> 00:09:38,182
The first lethal event was a rapid loss of cabin pressure, completed in about forty seconds, and the crew lost consciousness within seconds of that, before the more violent structural breakup even began.

104
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:50,062
The crew of Columbia is memorialized at the Space Mirror Memorial at Kennedy Space Center, their names added later that same year, alongside every other astronaut the American program has lost.

105
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:54,373
Eighteen died flying in space itself. Three more never left the ground at all.

106
00:09:54,373 --> 00:09:58,906
Twenty one people, five disasters, spanning almost forty years, and every single one of them made it back to Earth.

107
00:10:00,033 --> 00:10:10,560
Today they rest at the Kremlin Wall, at Arlington, at West Point, at the National Memorial Cemetery of the Pacific, and in hometowns from New Hampshire to South Carolina.

108
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:13,014
One honest footnote.

109
00:10:13,014 --> 00:10:20,898
In 1967, test pilot Michael Adams died when his X-15 rocket plane broke apart at over eighty kilometers.

110
00:10:20,898 --> 00:10:30,533
Under the strict definition, he never quite crossed the internationally recognized edge of space, so historians count him separately from the eighteen.

111
00:10:31,666 --> 00:10:35,701
And no, there are no human remains still floating in space.

112
00:10:35,701 --> 00:10:40,835
Every person who has ever died flying toward it has, eventually, been brought home.

113
00:10:41,966 --> 00:10:48,927
You may have also heard theories about Soviet cosmonauts who supposedly died and were erased before Gagarin ever flew.

114
00:10:48,927 --> 00:10:57,352
Serious historians consider that an unverified myth, not real history, and we've told that entire story, honestly, in another video on this channel.

115
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:07,027
If that lost cosmonauts myth got under your skin, wait until you see where the legend actually came from, and why most historians don't buy it.

116
00:11:07,027 --> 00:11:10,306
And subscribe to mindwired, so the next story finds you.

